<script>
/* =============== DATA =============== */
let CONFIG = null;
let POSTS = [
  {
    slug: 'prompt-caching',
    title: 'Caching prompts without burning money',
    date: '2026-10-13',
    excerpt: 'Where prompt caching actually saves tokens, where it silently misses, and how to measure the hit rate in production.',
    body: `
      <p>Most teams discover prompt caching the expensive way: a bill that doubled after a refactor moved a timestamp to the top of the system prompt. The fix is not clever. It is about where the stable part of your prompt ends.</p>
      <h2>The rule that matters</h2>
      <p>A cache hit needs an exact prefix match. Anything that changes early in the prompt, a timestamp, a request id, a shuffled tool list, invalidates everything after it.</p>
      <div class="callout"><b>Put volatile content last.</b> Static instructions and tool schemas first, user data after, and the request-specific bit at the very end.</div>
      <h2>Measure it</h2>
      <p>Log the cached and uncached token counts on every call. Without that number you are guessing.</p>
      <div class="codeblock" data-lang="python"><pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">log_cache</span>(usage):
    <span class="kw">hit</span> = usage.cache_read_input_tokens
    total = usage.input_tokens + hit
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"cache hit rate: {hit / total:.1%}"</span>)  <span class="cm"># alert if this drops</span></code></pre></div>
      <h2>Where it silently misses</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Tool lists generated from a dict whose order changes between processes.</li>
        <li>Few-shot examples sampled at random per request.</li>
        <li>Anything that includes the current time, even to the minute.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Fix the order, pin the examples, and the hit rate usually jumps within a day.</p>
    `
  },
  {
    slug: 'stateless-mcp',
    title: 'Stateless MCP: what changed in the transport',
    date: '2026-07-10',
    excerpt: 'Sessions are no longer the server\u2019s job. What you gain, what you give up, and how to keep long workflows working.',
    body: `
      <p>The newer transport spec moves MCP toward stateless request handling. Each call carries what the server needs, so any replica can answer it.</p>
      <h2>What you gain</h2>
      <p>Horizontal scaling becomes boring. No sticky sessions, no session store, and restarts stop being incidents.</p>
      <h2>What you give up</h2>
      <p>Anything you used to keep in a session now has to travel with the request or live in an external store. Plan for that before you migrate.</p>
      <div class="callout"><b>Rule of thumb:</b> if a tool needs more than one round trip of context, persist it explicitly with a short-lived key.</div>
      <h2>A minimal handler</h2>
      <div class="codeblock" data-lang="typescript"><pre><code><span class="kw">export async function</span> <span class="fn">handle</span>(req: <span class="nm">Request</span>) {
  <span class="kw">const</span> ctx = <span class="kw">await</span> <span class="fn">loadContext</span>(req.headers.get(<span class="st">"x-workflow-id"</span>))
  <span class="cm">// every call is self-contained, no in-memory session</span>
  <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">run</span>(req.body, ctx)
}</code></pre></div>
    `
  },
  {
    slug: 'queue-autoscaling',
    title: 'Autoscaling workers on queue depth, not CPU',
    date: '2026-05-04',
    excerpt: 'CPU is the wrong signal for background workers. Scale on backlog and time-to-drain instead.',
    body: `
      <p>Workers that pull from a queue often sit at 20 percent CPU while the backlog grows. CPU tells you the machine is busy, not that the work is late.</p>
      <h2>Scale on what users feel</h2>
      <p>Use the queue length divided by throughput per pod. That gives you an estimate of how long a new job will wait.</p>
      <div class="callout"><b>Target:</b> keep time-to-drain under your SLA, with a floor so you never scale to zero during a spike.</div>
      <h2>The metric</h2>
      <p>Expose queue depth to your autoscaler and let it scale on that. Keep the cooldown short for scale-up and long for scale-down.</p>
    `
  }
];

